Rich text, and the plain text that goes with it
===============================================

Phase 4 of doc/proposals/006-comunicaciones-unificadas.md.  One module,
modules/hooks/richtext.c, and one thing it exists to prevent.

IRC has no content type.  The thing not to do about that is invent a
dialect of control codes: every client would have to learn it, every one
that did not would show the codes, and the network would be split between
the two for ever.

So the format is negotiated, the body is bounded Markdown, and **every
client that did not negotiate it is sent the plain-text equivalent,
generated by the server**.  That last part is not a nicety.  It is the
whole design, and without it every decision after this one has to be made
twice.


HOW A CLIENT USES IT
====================

  CAP REQ :blacknode/richtext

advertised under CAP LS 302 with its value:

  blacknode/richtext=markdown

and then a message says what it is:

  @+blacknode/format=markdown PRIVMSG #room :hello **world**

A client that negotiated the capability is sent that line back as it
stands.  Everybody else is sent

  :nick!user@host PRIVMSG #room :hello world

with no tag on it -- the tag says "this body is Markdown", which is true
of one of those two bodies and a lie about the other, and a tag that is a
lie is worse than no tag.

The capability has to be negotiated to *send* Markdown as well as to
receive it.  A client that marks a message and never asked for the
capability has not agreed to anything, and its message goes out
unchanged.

CLIENTTAGDENY has to allow +blacknode/format or nothing is ever treated
as Markdown.  The module says so in the log at load time rather than
leaving it to be discovered from a bug report.


WHAT MARKDOWN MEANS HERE
========================

A bounded subset, and the bound is the server's.  A client that enforced
it would be one that could be replaced by a client that did not.

Carried:

  **bold**  *italic*  ~~strikethrough~~  `code`
  [text](https://example.org)
  > quotation
  - list

Refused, with FAIL-style text saying which rule was broken:

  - anything with < or > in it.  Markdown as a language allows raw HTML,
    and a client that renders what it is told is a client that can be
    made to render anything.  An angle bracket has no meaning in this
    subset, so refusing it costs nothing and closes the whole question.
  - IRC formatting codes.  A message carrying both would render
    differently in the two halves of the network no matter what is done
    here.
  - formatting nested more than three deep.  Three is a quoted list with
    a bold word in it; past that the text is not being formatted, it is
    being used to make a parser work hard.
  - a link that is not http:// or https://, or longer than 256
    characters.  A relative link means nothing without a document to be
    relative to, and every other scheme is a way to make a client do
    something rather than go somewhere.
  - a message that is only formatting.  Sending the Markdown to half the
    network and an empty line to the other half is worse than refusing
    it.

Long messages are draft/multiline, from phase 0.  Nothing here changes
for them: each piece is a message and each piece is rendered.


WHAT THE PLAIN TEXT LOOKS LIKE
==============================

What a person would have typed if they had not had formatting.  The
markers come off, and a link becomes its text followed by its target:

  hello **world** see [the docs](https://example.org/d)
  hello world see the docs <https://example.org/d>

unless the text is the target, in which case saying it twice helps
nobody:

  a bare [link](https://example.org) here
  a bare link <https://example.org> here

A quotation keeps its "> ", because that is how a quotation has been
written in plain text since long before Markdown.

The rendering never fails and never refuses.  By the time it runs the
text has been through the checks, and what is left has to produce
something for every input or half the network gets nothing.


WHERE THE WORK HAPPENS
======================

The module does not deliver either body.  It hooks
HOOK_MESSAGE_PRE_CHANNEL and HOOK_MESSAGE_PRE_PRIVATE, checks the
Markdown, writes the plain text into HookContext::hc_alt and names the
capability that chooses between them:

  ctx->hc_alt      the plain text
  ctx->hc_alt_cap  the capability a client needs to get the other one
  ctx->hc_alt_tag  a client tag not to relay with the plain text
  ctx->hc_alt_set  yes, I filled this in

and ircd/ircd_relay.c does the rest: the rich body to the clients that
have the capability and over the links, the plain body to everybody else,
and the plain body to whatever stores messages.

Mechanism in the core, policy in the module.  *How* a message reaches two
kinds of client is the relay's business; *what Markdown means* is this
module's.  Any other module that gives a message a content type gets the
same mechanism without asking for it.

The Markdown is what crosses P10, because the next server has the same
choice to make and cannot make it from the plain text.

What is **stored** is the plain text.  A transcript is read back by
whoever reads it, and the one body it can keep is the one everybody can
read; CHATHISTORY has one body per message and a stored Markdown would
be shown with its markup to every client that never asked for it.  The
formatting is lost to history, which is a real limitation and the right
side to err on.


COMPATIBILITY
=============

A client that negotiated nothing sees byte-identical lines to what it saw
before this module existed: plain text, no tags.  That is the rule the
whole roadmap runs on and this is the phase that would have broken it.
